---
import CopyBlock from "../components/CopyBlock.astro";
import QueueReplay from "../components/QueueReplay.astro";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const workerCommands = `cd worker
npm ci --ignore-scripts
npx wrangler login
npx wrangler deploy
npx wrangler secret put BUNPO_QUEUE_TOKEN
npx wrangler secret put BUNPO_QUEUE_READ_TOKEN`;

const serverCommands = `export BUNPO_QUEUE_TOKEN=$(openssl rand -hex 32)
export BUNPO_QUEUE_READ_TOKEN=$(openssl rand -hex 32)
bunpo-server --addr 127.0.0.1:8080 --data /var/lib/bunpo`;

const runnerExample = `find tests \\( -name 'test_*.py' -o -name '*_test.py' \\) | \\
  bunpo --queue "$BUNPO_QUEUE_URL" --build "$GITHUB_RUN_ID-$GITHUB_RUN_ATTEMPT" \\
    --index "$INDEX" --total "$TOTAL" --report junit.xml -- bash run-batch.sh`;
---

<Base
	title="Live queue (upcoming in 0.1.0) — Bunpō"
	description="Watch a recorded 8-machine live-queue build recover from a killed runner, and see how Bunpō runs on Cloudflare Durable Objects or bunpo-server. Upcoming in 0.1.0."
>
	<span class="eyebrow">live queue · upcoming in 0.1.0</span>
	<h1>Self-hosted live queue</h1>
	<p class="lead">
		Coming in <code>0.1.0</code> (not yet released; the current release is <code>v0.0.7</code>).{" "}
		<code>bunpo --queue</code> pulls batches of test files as each machine finishes, and reassigns
		leases when a runner is killed or loses a report.{" "}
		<a href="#replay">Watch the replay</a> or jump to{" "}
		<a href="#cloudflare">how it runs on Cloudflare</a>.
	</p>

	<section class="band" id="replay" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
		<div class="band-head">
			<div class="band-label">
				<strong>Replay</strong>
				<span>Recorded build · 8 machines, 120 files</span>
			</div>
			<div>
				<h2>Eight machines pull work, one gets killed, the build still passes.</h2>
				<p>
					This is a recording of a real local build from <code>bunpo-server</code> and{" "}
					<code>bunpo --queue</code> with sleeps in place of tests. Fast machines pull more batches,
					M6 starts late, M7 is <code>SIGKILL</code>ed mid-batch, and its expired files move to the
					survivors one by one.
				</p>
				<QueueReplay />
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="band" id="when-to-use">
		<div class="band-head">
			<div class="band-label">
				<strong>Static vs. queue</strong>
				<span>Pick the simpler tool</span>
			</div>
			<div>
				<h2>Use static splitting unless your machines drift apart.</h2>
				<p>
					Dynamic batching starts your test runner once per batch instead of once per machine. When
					identical machines start together, static splitting (<code>bunpo split</code> and{" "}
					<code>bunpo verify</code>) needs no server, avoids extra runner startups, and works on
					fork pull requests without secrets.
				</p>
				<div class="table-wrap">
					<table>
						<thead>
							<tr>
								<th>Condition</th>
								<th>Recommended mode</th>
							</tr>
						</thead>
						<tbody>
							<tr>
								<td>Identical CI machines starting together</td>
								<td><strong>Static</strong> (<code>split</code>, <code>record</code>, <code>verify</code>, <code>merge</code>)</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td>Fork pull requests without repository secrets</td>
								<td><strong>Static</strong></td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td>Mixed-speed runners (x64 + ARM64, noisy hosts)</td>
								<td><strong>Live queue</strong> (<code>--queue</code>)</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td>Runners starting minutes apart behind CI queues</td>
								<td><strong>Live queue</strong> (<code>--queue</code>)</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td>Spot or preemptible runners killed mid-build</td>
								<td><strong>Live queue</strong> (<code>--queue</code>)</td>
							</tr>
						</tbody>
					</table>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="band" id="cloudflare">
		<div class="band-head">
			<div class="band-label">
				<strong>How it runs on Cloudflare</strong>
				<span>Durable Objects + SQLite</span>
			</div>
			<div>
				<h2>One SQLite Durable Object per build, zero idle servers.</h2>
				<p>
					The Cloudflare Worker in <code>worker/</code> gives each build its own SQLite-backed
					Durable Object (<code>BuildDO</code>) so builds never contend with each other. Tables are
					created only when <code>init</code> persists; a <code>GET</code> on an unknown build ID
					writes zero rows.
				</p>
				<ul class="steps-list">
					<li>
						<span class="step-cmd">storage</span>
						<div>
							The test catalog is stored once in 1,000-file rows (<code>catalog_chunks</code>,
							about 68 KB/row for 60-byte paths, well under SQLite's 100 KB statement and 8 MB row
							limits). State changes append one row per decision to <code>event_log</code> across
							six event types, so replay never reads a clock.
						</div>
					</li>
					<li>
						<span class="step-cmd">leases</span>
						<div>
							Idle Durable Objects hibernate after about 10 s. <code>BuildDO</code> keeps ungraced
							lease deadlines in memory and throttles writes into one <code>meta</code> row
							(<code>lease_deadlines</code>) only when a heartbeat pushes a lease past{" "}
							<code>persisted + floor(ttl / 2)</code> (25 to 27 <code>meta</code> writes in a
							64-machine 10-minute build). On wake, active leases receive{" "}
							<code>floor(ttl / 2)</code> grace without persisting the graced value, so leases
							never expire early and dead runners still expire within half a TTL.
						</div>
					</li>
					<li>
						<span class="step-cmd">alarms</span>
						<div>
							Each <code>BuildDO</code> schedules its lifecycle alarm in the same synchronous turn
							as the SQLite write: first at <code>build_deadline</code> while running, then 7 days
							after completion to call <code>ctx.storage.deleteAll()</code> and free storage.
						</div>
					</li>
					<li>
						<span class="step-cmd">assets</span>
						<div>
							A separate SQLite <code>BuildIndex</code> object keeps up to 200 recent build
							summaries (at most two inline RPCs per build). Dashboard files (<code>/</code> and{" "}
							<code>/dashboard/*</code>) are served directly by Workers Static Assets at 0 Worker
							requests and 0 Durable Object wakes.
						</div>
					</li>
				</ul>

				<div class="table-wrap" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
					<table>
						<thead>
							<tr>
								<th>Measured on Cloudflare Workers Free (2026-11-25)</th>
								<th>Result</th>
							</tr>
						</thead>
						<tbody>
							<tr>
								<td>Two full trial sessions (929 Worker requests total)</td>
								<td>0 errors; 21 builds of 20,000 files passed (including 40 builds started over 50 s, 360 requests all HTTP 200)</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td>Heartbeats across DO hibernation</td>
								<td>8 machines / 400 files held leases up to 30,132 ms on a 15 s TTL; 1 machine held a 150,089 ms lease with 20 s heartbeats; 114 recorded heartbeats &le; 1 ms CPU</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td><code>BuildDO</code> CPU (Run 2 sustained load)</td>
								<td><code>init</code>: 14 to 46 ms at 5,000 files (median 24 ms), 31 to 73 ms at 20,000 (median 49 ms); <code>status</code> up to 54 ms; deadline <code>alarm</code> 7 to 43 ms</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td>Worker <code>fetch</code> CPU</td>
								<td>Up to 8 ms on 20,000-file <code>init</code> requests (1.04 MB body) and 6 ms on 5,000-file builds (0.26 MB); 4 MiB bodies untested</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td>Default <code>MAX_TESTS</code> and trace sampling</td>
								<td><code>MAX_TESTS</code> defaults to 5,000 (up to 20,000) for Worker <code>fetch</code> CPU margin and 100,000 SQLite rows written/day; traces sampled at 5%, logs at 100%</td>
							</tr>
						</tbody>
					</table>
				</div>

				<p class="small muted">
					Cloudflare's documentation disagrees on the Free-plan CPU limit inside a Durable Object:
					the Durable Objects limits table lists 30 s default with no Free value, while its FAQ
					says Durable Objects share Workers' per-invocation CPU limits and the Workers limits page
					gives 10 ms on Free. Across both trials, 130 Durable Object calls exceeded 10 ms (up to
					73 ms) and none was stopped. If a stricter limit is ever enforced, the object throws, the
					Worker answers <code>503 storage_error</code>, and runners retry for 2 minutes before
					exiting 1, never producing a false pass. Full raw numbers and untested gaps live in{" "}
					<a
						href="https://github.com/nuggocto/bunpo/blob/shrek/docs/trials/2026-11-25-cloudflare-free-trial.md"
						target="_blank"
						rel="noreferrer"
					>
						docs/trials/2026-11-25-cloudflare-free-trial.md
					</a>
					.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="band" id="servers">
		<div class="band-head">
			<div class="band-label">
				<strong>Two servers</strong>
				<span>Same protocol &amp; UI</span>
			</div>
			<div>
				<h2>Cloudflare Worker or standalone Go binary.</h2>
				<p>
					Both backends speak the exact same HTTP JSON v1 protocol and serve the same static
					read-only web dashboard (recent builds, per-machine lease timeline, slowest files,
					and attempt history). Nothing is coupled to a vendor.
				</p>
				<CopyBlock label="Cloudflare Worker (zero-ops, SQLite Durable Objects)" code={workerCommands} />
				<CopyBlock label="Standalone Go server (put TLS in front)" code={serverCommands} />
				<p class="small muted">
					Full setup, systemd unit, backups, token rotation, and Cloudflare Free-plan limits live
					in{" "}
					<a
						href="https://github.com/nuggocto/bunpo/blob/shrek/docs/self-hosting.md"
						target="_blank"
						rel="noreferrer"
					>
						docs/self-hosting.md
					</a>
					.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="band" id="ci-usage">
		<div class="band-head">
			<div class="band-label">
				<strong>In CI</strong>
				<span>Runner &amp; status job</span>
			</div>
			<div>
				<h2>One worker command per machine, one status job at the end.</h2>
				<p>
					Each machine pipes its test list into <code>bunpo --queue</code> with{" "}
					<code>BUNPO_QUEUE_TOKEN</code>. Bunpō strips queue tokens from the child test command,
					sends heartbeats at <code>lease_ttl / 3</code>, and reports results per batch. A final
					job runs <code>bunpo --queue ... --status</code> with{" "}
					<code>BUNPO_QUEUE_READ_TOKEN</code> to print the verdict and write{" "}
					<code>report-0.json</code> for <code>bunpo merge</code>.
				</p>
				<CopyBlock label="Worker step (pytest example)" code={runnerExample} />
				<p class="small">
					See the <strong>Live queue</strong> section in any{" "}
					<a href="/guides">runner guide</a> (pytest, Jest, Vitest, RSpec, Playwright), check all
					flags in the <a href="/cli">CLI reference</a>, or read the{" "}
					<a
						href="https://github.com/nuggocto/bunpo/blob/shrek/docs/queue-protocol.md"
						target="_blank"
						rel="noreferrer"
					>
						HTTP protocol specification
					</a>
					.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</Base>
